  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1023 PM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nAnother clear and chilly night on the way. While winds are lighter\nthan last night, the dewpoints are running a little higher, and\nthis should keep lowland temperatures 2 or 3 degrees higher than\nlast night/Sunday morning. So, no new freeze areas are expected.\n\nA fast-moving upper level trough looks to move through New Mexico\nMonday night into Tuesday. Coarser-scale models (GFS, ECMWF) have\ntrended towards expanding the light precip coverage further \nsouth, but the RRFS has tapped the brakes with regards to QPF. The\nHRRR remains unimpressed with precip chances. Leaned on GFS/ECMWF\nfor now, with the lighter QPF amounts (0.01 to 0.05 inches), and\nslight chance PoPs touching El Paso. Scattered snow showers are\nexpected in the Sacramento Mountains late Monday night, but\naccumulations will generally be under an inch.\n\nFor the week ahead, quiet weather looks to prevail. Everything has\ntrended weaker with the potential cutoff low dropping into Mexico.\nSo the main headline is just cool temperatures (highs 3-6 degrees\nbelow normal) through mid-week, warming back up to near normal\nThursday and Friday. Tuesday night may see \"first freeze\" areas\nexpand in the lowland a bit more, with Las Cruces at risk, and\nrural areas of El Paso and Hudspeth Counties, including the Upper\nand Lower Valleys.",
